answer.text:
Neglecting channel-length modulation and transistor capacitances, and assuming the drain of \(M_2\) is tied to node \(X\) (diode-connected, as drawn), define

\[
g_{\rm eff}=\frac{g_{m2}g_{m3}}{g_{m2}+g_{m3}} .
\]

The open-loop small-signal voltage gain from \(P\) to \(V_{\rm out}\) is

\[
A_v=\left.\frac{v_{\rm out}}{v_P}\right|_{R_F\to\infty}
=
-\frac{g_{m1}R_{D1}\,g_{\rm eff}R_{D2}}{1+g_{\rm eff}R_{D1}} .
\]

With the shunt feedback resistor \(R_F\) and output resistance \(R_o\simeq R_{D2}\), the low-frequency active input resistance is

\[
\boxed{
R_{\rm in}
=
\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}{1- A_v}
=
\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}
{1+\dfrac{g_{m1}R_{D1}g_{\rm eff}R_{D2}}{1+g_{\rm eff}R_{D1}}}
}
\]

or, equivalently,

\[
\boxed{
R_{\rm in}
=
(R_F+R_{D2})\,
\frac{1+g_{\rm eff}R_{D1}}
{1+g_{\rm eff}R_{D1}+g_{m1}R_{D1}g_{\rm eff}R_{D2}}
}
\]

with
\[
g_{\rm eff}=\frac{g_{m2}g_{m3}}{g_{m2}+g_{m3}}.
\]

The total input impedance, including the input capacitor \(C_{\rm in}\), is

\[
\boxed{
Z_{\rm in}(s)
=
R_{\rm in}\parallel \frac{1}{sC_{\rm in}}
=
\left(
sC_{\rm in}
+
\frac{1+|A_v|}{R_F+R_{D2}}
\right)^{-1},
\qquad s=j\omega .
}
\]

For \(g_{m2}=g_{m3}=g_m\), this becomes

\[
|A_v|
=
\frac{g_{m1}R_{D1}\,g_mR_{D2}}
{2+g_mR_{D1}},
\qquad
R_{\rm in}
=
\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}
{1+\dfrac{g_{m1}R_{D1}g_mR_{D2}}{2+g_mR_{D1}}}.
\]

In the usual high-gain approximation \(g_mR_{D1}\gg2\) and \(g_{m1}R_{D2}\gg1\),

\[
\boxed{
R_{\rm in}\approx \frac{R_F}{1+g_{m1}R_{D2}},
\qquad
Z_{\rm in}\approx
\left(
sC_{\rm in}
+
\frac{1+g_{m1}R_{D2}}{R_F}
\right)^{-1}.
}
\]

If the question asks only for the input resistance looking into the amplifier, use the \(R_{\rm in}\) expression above; include \(C_{\rm in}\) only when the total input impedance at the source node is required.

Let the small-signal input-node voltage be \(v_p\), and define the voltage gain from the input node to the output as

\[
A_v(s)=\frac{v_{out}}{v_p}
\]

including its sign.

Since the MOS gate draws negligible current, the input current flows only into \(C_{in}\) and through \(R_F\). Thus,

\[
i_{in}=sC_{in}v_p+\frac{v_p-v_{out}}{R_F}
\]

\[
i_{in}=v_p\left[sC_{in}+\frac{1-A_v(s)}{R_F}\right]
\]

So the input impedance is

\[
\boxed{
Z_{in}(s)=\frac{v_p}{i_{in}}
=
\frac{1}{sC_{in}+\dfrac{1-A_v(s)}{R_F}}
}
\]

or equivalently,

\[
\boxed{
Z_{in}(s)=\frac{R_F}{1-A_v(s)+sR_FC_{in}}
}
\]

If the amplifier is inverting, i.e.

\[
A_v(s)=-A(s),
\]

then

\[
\boxed{
Z_{in}(s)=\frac{R_F}{1+A(s)+sR_FC_{in}}
}
\]

and at low frequency,

\[
\boxed{
R_{in}\approx \frac{R_F}{1+A_0}
}
\]

where \(A_0\) is the midband magnitude of the voltage gain from node \(P\) to \(V_{out}\).

Assume an ideal tail-current source, \(V_{DD}\) and \(V_b\) are ac grounds, and transistor output resistances are neglected.

For a test voltage \(v_p\) at \(P\),
\[
v_x=-g_{m1}R_{D1}v_p.
\]

The single-ended transconductance of the \(M_2\)-\(M_3\) pair is
\[
g_{md}=\frac{g_{m2}g_{m3}}{g_{m2}+g_{m3}}.
\]
Thus, with \(R_F\) disconnected,
\[
\frac{v_{\text{out}}}{v_p}=-A_0,
\qquad
A_0=g_{m1}R_{D1}\,g_{md}R_{D2}.
\]

For matched \(M_2\) and \(M_3\), \(g_{m2}=g_{m3}=g_{mp}\), so
\[
A_0=\frac{1}{2}g_{m1}g_{mp}R_{D1}R_{D2}.
\]

Including the finite output resistance \(R_{D2}\), the resistance looking into \(P\), excluding \(C_{\text{in}}\), is
\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{in}}
=\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}{1+A_0}
}
\]
or, for matched \(M_2,M_3\),
\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{in}}
=
\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}
{1+\dfrac{1}{2}g_{m1}g_{mp}R_{D1}R_{D2}}
}.
\]

Usually \(R_F\gg R_{D2}\), giving the familiar approximation
\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{in}}\simeq
\frac{R_F}
{1+\dfrac{1}{2}g_{m1}g_{mp}R_{D1}R_{D2}}
}.
\]

Including the input capacitance, the impedance seen by \(I_{\text{in}}\) is
\[
\boxed{
Z_{\text{in}}(s)
=R_{\text{in}}\parallel\frac{1}{sC_{\text{in}}}
=
\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}
{1+A_0+sC_{\text{in}}(R_F+R_{D2})}
}.
\]

Assume small-signal operation, \(V_{DD}\) and \(V_b\) are ac grounds, \(I_{SS}\) is ideal, and \(r_o\) is neglected.

Let a test voltage \(v_p\) be applied at node \(P\). For \(M_1\),

\[
v_x=-g_{m1}R_{D1}v_p .
\]

The \(M_2\)-\(M_3\) pMOS pair converts \(v_x\) to an output current with effective transconductance

\[
g_{md}=\frac{g_{m2}g_{m3}}{g_{m2}+g_{m3}} .
\]

Hence the open-loop gain from \(P\) to the output is

\[
A_0\equiv \left.\frac{v_{\text{out}}}{v_p}\right|_{R_F\text{ open}}
=-g_{m1}R_{D1}g_{md}R_{D2}.
\]

For matched \(M_2\) and \(M_3\),

\[
g_{md}=\frac{g_{m2}}{2},
\qquad
A_0=-\frac{1}{2}g_{m1}g_{m2}R_{D1}R_{D2}.
\]

Including the loading of the output by \(R_F\), the input admittance is

\[
Y_{\text{in}}(s)
=
sC_{\text{in}}
+
\frac{1-A_0}{R_F+R_{D2}}.
\]

Therefore,

\[
\boxed{
Z_{\text{in}}(s)
=
\left[
sC_{\text{in}}
+
\frac{1+g_{m1}R_{D1}g_{md}R_{D2}}
{R_F+R_{D2}}
\right]^{-1}
}
\]

or, for matched \(M_2\) and \(M_3\),

\[
\boxed{
Z_{\text{in}}(s)
=
\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}
{
1+\dfrac{1}{2}g_{m1}g_{m2}R_{D1}R_{D2}
+sC_{\text{in}}(R_F+R_{D2})
}
}.
\]

Thus the low-frequency input resistance is

\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{in}}
=
\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}
{1+\dfrac{1}{2}g_{m1}g_{m2}R_{D1}R_{D2}}
}.
\]

If \(R_F\gg R_{D2}\), the commonly used approximation is

\[
\boxed{
Z_{\text{in}}(s)\approx
\frac{R_F}
{1+\dfrac{1}{2}g_{m1}g_{m2}R_{D1}R_{D2}
+sR_FC_{\text{in}}}
}
\]

and hence

\[
R_{\text{in}}\approx
\frac{R_F}
{1+\dfrac{1}{2}g_{m1}g_{m2}R_{D1}R_{D2}}.
\]

Assume small-signal operation, ideal \(I_{SS}\), and negligible MOS output conductances and body effect. Both \(V_{DD}\) and \(V_b\) are AC grounds.

Let \(v_i=v_P\). The first stage gives
\[
v_x=-g_{m1}R_{D1}v_i.
\]

Since the total small-signal source current of \(M_2,M_3\) is zero, their effective transconductance from \(v_x\) to the output is
\[
G_m=\frac{g_{m2}g_{m3}}{g_{m2}+g_{m3}}.
\]
For matched \(M_2,M_3\), this becomes \(G_m=g_m/2\).

Define the magnitude of the **unloaded forward voltage gain**
\[
A_0=g_{m1}R_{D1}G_mR_{D2}.
\]
The output is then equivalent to a voltage source \(-A_0v_i\) in series with \(R_{D2}\). Consequently, the current entering the feedback path is
\[
i_f=\frac{v_i-(-A_0v_i)}{R_F+R_{D2}}.
\]

Thus the resistance labeled \(R_{\mathrm{in}}\), excluding \(C_{\mathrm{in}}\), is
\[
\boxed{
R_{\mathrm{in}}
=\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}
{1+g_{m1}R_{D1}
\left(\dfrac{g_{m2}g_{m3}}{g_{m2}+g_{m3}}\right)R_{D2}}
}.
\]

For matched \(M_2,M_3\),
\[
\boxed{
R_{\mathrm{in}}
=\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}
{1+\frac12 g_{m1}g_mR_{D1}R_{D2}}
}.
\]

**Including the shown input capacitance**, the impedance seen by \(I_{\mathrm{in}}\) is
\[
\boxed{
Z_{\mathrm{in}}(s)
=R_{\mathrm{in}}\parallel\frac{1}{sC_{\mathrm{in}}}
=\frac{R_{\mathrm{in}}}{1+sR_{\mathrm{in}}C_{\mathrm{in}}}
}.
\]

If \(R_F\gg R_{D2}\), the familiar approximation is \(R_{\mathrm{in}}\approx R_F/(1+A_0)\).

Assume ideal bias sources, negligible MOS output conductances (\(r_o\to\infty\)), and no intrinsic transistor capacitances. \(V_{DD}\) and \(V_b\) are ac grounds.

The effective transconductance from \(X\) to the output through \(M_2,M_3\) is
\[
G_m=\frac{g_{m2}g_{m3}}{g_{m2}+g_{m3}},
\]
which equals \(g_m/2\) for matched devices.

Apply a test voltage \(v_p\) at the input. Then
\[
v_x=-g_{m1}R_{D1}v_p.
\]
Writing \(i_F=(v_p-v_{\text{out}})/R_F\), output-node KCL gives
\[
\frac{v_{\text{out}}}{R_{D2}}=G_m v_x+i_F.
\]
Substituting \(v_{\text{out}}=v_p-i_FR_F\) yields
\[
v_p\left(1+g_{m1}R_{D1}G_mR_{D2}\right)
=i_F(R_F+R_{D2}).
\]

Thus, **the resistance looking right into \(P\)**, excluding \(C_{\text{in}}\), is
\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{in}}
=\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}
{1+g_{m1}R_{D1}G_mR_{D2}}
}.
\]
For matched \(M_2,M_3\),
\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{in}}
=\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}
{1+\frac12 g_{m1}g_mR_{D1}R_{D2}}
}.
\]

**Including \(C_{\text{in}}\)**, the impedance seen by the input current source is
\[
\boxed{
Z_{\text{in}}(s)
=R_{\text{in}}\parallel\frac{1}{sC_{\text{in}}}
=\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}
{1+g_{m1}R_{D1}G_mR_{D2}
+sC_{\text{in}}(R_F+R_{D2})}
},\qquad s=j\omega.
\]

Assume an ideal bias current source and neglect MOS output resistances and intrinsic capacitances. \(V_{DD}\) and \(V_b\) are AC grounds.

Because the total current in \(M_2,M_3\) is fixed, their effective transconductance from \(v_x\) to the current injected into the output is
\[
g_{\rm eq}=\frac{g_{m2}g_{m3}}{g_{m2}+g_{m3}}
\quad \left(=\frac{g_m}{2}\text{ for a matched pair}\right).
\]

Apply a test voltage \(v_p\), and let \(i\) enter the circuit **to the right of \(C_{\rm in}\)**. Then
\[
v_x=-g_{m1}R_{D1}v_p,\qquad
v_o=R_{D2}(i+g_{\rm eq}v_x),\qquad
v_p=v_o+iR_F.
\]
Combining these gives
\[
\boxed{
R_{\rm in}=\frac{v_p}{i}
=\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}
{1+g_{m1}R_{D1}g_{\rm eq}R_{D2}}
}.
\]

For matched \(M_2,M_3\), this becomes
\[
\boxed{
R_{\rm in}=
\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}
{1+\frac12 g_{m1}g_mR_{D1}R_{D2}}
}.
\]

Including the shown input capacitor, the total impedance seen by \(I_{\rm in}\) is
\[
\boxed{
Z_{\rm in}(s)
=R_{\rm in}\parallel\frac{1}{sC_{\rm in}}
=\frac{R_{\rm in}}{1+sR_{\rm in}C_{\rm in}}
}.
\]

Assume low frequency, negligible gate current, and neglect channel-length modulation/body effect. \(V_{DD}\) and \(V_b\) are ac grounds and \(I_{SS}\) only sets bias.

Node \(X\):

\[
v_X=-g_{m1}R_{D1}v_P .
\]

For the source-coupled pair \(M_2,M_3\), with the gate of \(M_3\) at ac ground,

\[
(g_{m2}+g_{m3})v_S=g_{m2}v_X,
\qquad
i_o=g_{m3}v_S
\]

so

\[
i_o=\frac{g_{m2}g_{m3}}{g_{m2}+g_{m3}}v_X .
\]

Thus the open-circuit voltage gain from \(P\) to \(V_{out}\) is

\[
A_v=\frac{v_{out}}{v_P}
=
-g_{m1}R_{D1}
\left(\frac{g_{m2}g_{m3}}{g_{m2}+g_{m3}}\right)R_{D2}
\equiv -A_0 .
\]

The feedback resistor is reflected to the input as

\[
Z_F=\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}{1-A_v}
=
\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}{1+A_0}.
\]

Therefore the impedance seen by \(I_{in}\) at node \(P\) is

\[
\boxed{
Z_{in}(s)=
\left[
\frac{1}{R_{in}}
+sC_{in}
+
\frac{
1+g_{m1}R_{D1}
\left(\dfrac{g_{m2}g_{m3}}{g_{m2}+g_{m3}}\right)R_{D2}
}{
R_F+R_{D2}
}
\right]^{-1}
}
\]

Equivalently, \(Z_{in}(s)=R_{eq}/(1+sR_{eq}C_{in})\), where \(R_{eq}\) is the parallel combination above.

If \(R_F\gg R_{D2}\), this reduces to the usual Miller approximation

\[
Z_{in}(s)\approx
\left[
\frac{1}{R_{in}}+sC_{in}+
\frac{1+A_0}{R_F}
\right]^{-1}.
\]

Let \(v_i=v_P\), \(v_x\) be the drain voltage of \(M_1\), and \(v_o=v_{\text{out}}\). Assume the usual small-signal model: the MOS transistors have infinite \(r_o\), \(V_b\) is an ac ground, and the ideal bias current source has zero incremental current.

Since the gate current of \(M_1\) is zero,

\[
i_{\text{in}}(s)=sC_{\text{in}}v_i+\frac{v_i-v_o}{R_F}.
\]

Thus, in the most general form, if

\[
G(s)\equiv \frac{v_o}{v_i},
\]

then

\[
\boxed{
Z_{\text{in}}(s)
=\left[sC_{\text{in}}+\frac{1-G(s)}{R_F}\right]^{-1}
}
\]

### For the circuit as drawn

Interpreting \(M_2\) as diode-connected, with its gate and drain at \(X\), and \(M_2,M_3\) as the common-source PMOS pair, define

\[
k=\frac{g_{m2}g_{m3}}{g_{m2}+g_{m3}}.
\]

Small-signal analysis gives

\[
\frac{v_x}{v_i}
=-A_x,
\qquad
A_x=\frac{g_{m1}}{1/R_{D1}+k}.
\]

With \(R_F\) disconnected, the open-loop gain magnitude is

\[
A_o=kA_xR_{D2}
=
\boxed{
\frac{g_{m1}g_{m2}g_{m3}R_{D1}R_{D2}}
{g_{m2}+g_{m3}+R_{D1}g_{m2}g_{m3}}
}.
\]

The output resistance with \(r_o\to\infty\) is \(R_{D2}\). Therefore,

\[
\boxed{
Z_{\text{in}}(s)=
\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}
{1+A_o+s(R_F+R_{D2})C_{\text{in}}}
}
\]

and the dc input resistance is

\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{in}}
=
\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}
{1+
\dfrac{g_{m1}g_{m2}g_{m3}R_{D1}R_{D2}}
{g_{m2}+g_{m3}+R_{D1}g_{m2}g_{m3}}
}
}.
\]

If \(R_F\gg R_{D2}\), this reduces to the commonly used feedback result

\[
Z_{\text{in}}(s)\approx
\frac{R_F}{1+A_o+sR_FC_{\text{in}}}.
\]

A numerical value cannot be obtained without the transistor \(g_m\)'s and bias information.

Looking into node \(P\) **excluding \(C_{in}\)**, the gate of \(M_1\) draws no current, so the only input current is through \(R_F\).

Let

\[
A_v \equiv \frac{v_{out}}{v_P}
\]

be the low-frequency voltage gain from \(P\) to \(V_{out}\). For the polarity shown, \(A_v<0\) negative feedback. Apply a test voltage \(v_t\) at \(P\):

\[
i_t=\frac{v_t-v_{out}}{R_F}
=\frac{v_t(1-A_v)}{R_F}
\]

so

\[
\boxed{R_{in}=\frac{v_t}{i_t}=\frac{R_F}{1-A_v}}
\]

Since \(A_v=-|A_v|\) for this circuit,

\[
\boxed{R_{in}=\frac{R_F}{1+|A_v|}}
\]

If \(M_2/M_3\) are approximately a unity-gain buffer/level shifter and \(r_o\) is neglected,

\[
|A_v|\approx g_{m1}R_{D1}
\]

so

\[
\boxed{R_{in}\approx \frac{R_F}{1+g_{m1}R_{D1}}
\approx \frac{R_F}{g_{m1}R_{D1}}\quad (g_{m1}R_{D1}\gg1)}
\]

Including the explicit input capacitor,

\[
\boxed{Z_{in}(s)=R_{in}\parallel \frac{1}{sC_{in}}
=\frac{R_{in}}{1+sR_{in}C_{in}}}
\]

with \(R_{in}\) given above. At low frequency \(Z_{in}\approx R_{in}\); at high frequency \(C_{in}\) dominates.
